## 12. Benchmark #4 — Runtime / computational efficiency

Since the whole point of extending ABC to single-cell data is practicality at scale (potentially thousands of cells or cell clusters, each needing its own scoring run), the authors also benchmarked the speed of their software implementation, called **STARE**.

- They compared their own optimized implementation against the **original ABC pipeline code released by Fulco et al.**, tested on a task of scoring **155,976 candidate enhancers against 24,586 genes within a 10-megabase (Mb) window**, in K562 cells, run with a varying number of parallel processor cores. Each measurement was the mean of **five separate runs**.
- Separately, they measured runtime **per activity column** (i.e., per additional cell type / sample processed in the same batch), on the single-cell heart dataset (286,777 candidate enhancers, 55,765 genes, 5 Mb window, 818 TFs assessed), including the time needed to write and compress output files. Each measurement was the mean of **three runs**.
- **Key finding:** Runtime per additional activity column **decreases** the more columns (cell types/samples) you process together in a single batch run — meaning STARE is specifically optimized for the realistic single-cell use case of scoring many cell types/clusters at once, rather than one at a time, and can process large datasets "in a few minutes" once batched efficiently.

