## 13. Summary of what gABC actually changed, in one paragraph

The original ABC model scored enhancer-gene pairs using (enhancer activity) × (contact frequency), normalized per gene. The gABC model makes two changes: first, it divides an enhancer's activity proportionally across every gene it's near (weighted by relative contact frequency to each), so a promiscuous enhancer near many genes no longer gets to "double count" its full activity for each one; second, it sums contact information across *every* annotated transcription start site of a gene instead of arbitrarily picking just one. These two changes, especially the TSS fix, produced a substantial and statistically significant average improvement of about 0.107 AUPRC points over three independent CRISPR-validated benchmark datasets, beat a large sequence-based deep learning model (Enformer), recovered more real eQTL-gene links from GTEx data, and — when combined with a companion tool (STARE) that summarizes transcription factor binding affinities per gene — produced better gene expression prediction models than either the original ABC score or a competing co-accessibility-based method, when applied to real single-cell heart tissue data. A key practical side-finding was that using just one chromatin accessibility assay (DNase-seq or ATAC-seq) instead of the two required by the original method gives nearly identical accuracy, which is what makes gABC realistically usable on single-cell data in the first place (since measuring two separate modalities in the same individual cell is very difficult with current technology).
